A Greedy algorithm is an algorithmic approach that makes the best possible choice at each step without considering future consequences. The idea is that these local optimal choices will lead to a globally optimal solution for certain problems.
Greedy algorithms are used because they are:
Simple to design and implement.
Fast, often running in O(n) or O(nlogn) time.
Efficient for problems that satisfy the greedy-choice property and optimal substructure.
However, greedy algorithms do not always produce the optimal solution for every problem.
Example: 
Suppose you have coins of ₹10, ₹5, ₹2, and ₹1, and you need to make ₹18.
public class CoinChange {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] coins = {10, 5, 2, 1};
        int amount = 18;
        int count = 0;
        System.out.println("Coins used:");
        for (int coin : coins) {
            while (amount >= coin) {
                System.out.print(coin + " ");
                amount -= coin;
                count++;
            }
        }
        System.out.println("\nMinimum number of coins = " + count);
    }
}
